  "name": "Question 10",
  "problem": "Given the polynomial $P(x)=x(x+3)^{4}(x-1),$ find the solution of $P(x)>0.$",
  "worked_solution": "To solve the inequality $P(x)>0,$ we need to find the values of $x$ for which the curve $y=P(x)$ lies above the $x$-axis.\n\nThe roots of the polynomial $P(x)=x(x+3)^{4}(x-1)$ are $x=-3,x=0$ and $x=1,$ where\n\n- $x=-3$ has multiplicity $4,$ and\n\n- $x=0$ and $x=1$ are simple roots.\n\nAlso, $P(x)$ is a polynomial of degree $6$ with a positive leading coefficient. So, we can graph the curve as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007649/assets/6938cb283f25ccc325d7f50fcd793f1b9928025218bb6e9ca08f2d3941d16690.png)\n\nSince we are interested in solving $P(x)>0,$ we look for the part of the curve that is above the $x$-axis. \n\nBased on the graph, the solution is $x\\in (-\\infty ,-3)\\cup (-3,0)\\cup (1,\\infty ).$",
